INT. OBSERVATORY LIBRARY - NIGHT

A circular room beneath a copper dome, shelves climbing in tiers around a wrought-iron gallery. A rolling ladder on a brass track. A reading table, a green banker's lamp, a half-drunk glass of sherry. A body-sized shape of dust where a rug has been recently pulled aside.

PROFESSOR ALISTAIR GREYSON-MBEKI (60s, tweed jacket, bow tie) lies on the floor, face turned toward the dome. His eyes are open. A small wooden stool tilts nearby, one leg snapped.

INSPECTOR LENORE QUIGLEY-ABARA (40s, black wool coat, notebook) crouches at his shoulder, gloved fingers hovering over his collar. Behind her, in the doorway, stands the housekeeper MRS. PRUNELLA DOUGHTY (60s, apron, white curls, hands clasped tight), who is trying not to look.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
When did you find him?

PRUNELLA
Half past nine, Inspector. I came in to turn down the lamp.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
And the door?

PRUNELLA
Open. Always open. He hates a closed door.

Quigley-Abara stands. Walks the circumference of the room slowly, trailing a hand along the shelves. At the ladder she stops, tests the brass track with a thumbnail, and lifts a smear of something pale.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
Chalk.

PRUNELLA
For the blackboard.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
There's no blackboard in this room.

PRUNELLA
No. I suppose there isn't.

The Inspector turns. Prunella has moved a little way into the room, and now stands with one hand on the reading table, steadying herself. The other hand has found the sherry glass and turned it a quarter turn to hide a lipstick mark on its rim.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
Mrs. Doughty.

PRUNELLA
Yes, Inspector?

QUIGLEY-ABARA
Do you take sherry?

PRUNELLA
Never.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
That's your shade of pink, though.

Prunella's mouth works. Slowly she lifts her chin, as if resolving to be brave about something she hasn't yet been accused of.

PRUNELLA
He asked me to sit with him.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
Did he?

PRUNELLA
Last night. It was his birthday. He had no one.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
And you drank sherry together.

PRUNELLA
One glass.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
And then?

PRUNELLA
And then I went to bed.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
And he climbed the ladder, in the dark, at seventy-one, to reach the Almagest.

PRUNELLA
He always did that.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
Not with a stool.

She crosses to the broken stool and rights it. Looks at the snapped leg: the wood is white and fresh, with a clean diagonal where a saw has been.

QUIGLEY-ABARA (CONT'D)
That's not a fall.

PRUNELLA
(very quietly)
No.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
Who sawed it?

PRUNELLA
I did.

The Inspector does not move. The lamp hums. In the high copper dome, the wind lifts a flap of loose metal and lets it fall with a soft clang.

PRUNELLA (CONT'D)
He was going to sell the telescope.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
To whom?

PRUNELLA
To a man who'd put it in a hotel lobby.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
And that was cause enough.

PRUNELLA
Forty years I've polished that brass.

She sits down on the reading chair. Smooths her apron. Folds her hands. Quigley-Abara closes the notebook, comes round the table, and takes the sherry glass by its stem. She sets it in an evidence bag. The sound of the bag zip is very loud.

QUIGLEY-ABARA
Thank you, Mrs. Doughty.

PRUNELLA
Will there be tea?

QUIGLEY-ABARA
There will be tea.

FADE OUT.
